// Concept texts, batch 11 (names THANATOPHILE to THE VORAREPHILE).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["THANATOPHILE", "F", "A strong fascination with death, dead bodies, and the end of life.", "The pull can feel dark, intense, and hard to explain to others. For some people it is a sexual interest, which can bring shame or secrecy.", "Frequent talk or searches about death, graves, or dead bodies, often in private.", "Curiosity about mortality is common and can be thoughtful. A fixation that brings distress or drives risky behavior deserves help from a professional.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis. If you are having thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, or call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["THANATOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong, persistent fear of dying or of one's own death.", "The thought of death can bring panic, a racing heart, or a wave of dread. It can return at night and feel impossible to shake.", "Frequent reassurance seeking, checking for signs of illness, or avoiding anything linked to death.", "Thinking about death is a normal part of being human. When fear fills daily life, it is worth talking with a counselor who can help.", "Fear of death often responds well to treatment, such as therapy with a trained counselor. If you are having thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, or call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["THE PITY PLOYER", "C", "A person who uses pity or a show of hardship to get what they want from others.", "Others may feel guilty, pressed, or responsible for fixing every problem. Over time, they may feel used and tired.", "Repeated stories of hardship that are used to win favors, to avoid accountability, or to end arguments.", "Real hardship deserves care, and asking for help is not manipulation. The pattern matters when pity is used to control others or to avoid responsibility.", ""],
  ["THE RIGID", "R", "A person who holds tightly to rules, routines, or their own way of doing things.", "Change can feel threatening, and it may bring tension or irritation. Rigid thinking can feel safe, even when it is costly.", "Refusing new plans, insisting on one correct method, and getting upset when routines shift.", "Structure can be healthy and helpful. Rigidity becomes a problem when it blocks cooperation, learning, or close relationships.", ""],
  ["THOUGHT LABELING", "K", "A practice of naming a passing thought as a thought, instead of treating it as fact.", "Naming a thought can create a little space between you and it. The thought may still be uncomfortable, but it can feel less solid.", "Pausing mid-conversation, or saying 'I am having the thought that...' in a calm voice.", "Labeling a thought is not dismissing it. It helps you see the thought clearly so it is less likely to steer your choices.", ""],
  ["THOUGHTS", "T", "The ongoing stream of ideas, images, and words that moves through the mind.", "Thoughts can feel like facts, especially when they repeat or come with strong emotion. Many thoughts are passing and do not need action.", "Staring into space, seeming distracted, or going quiet while working through something.", "Having a thought does not mean it is true or that you want it to happen. Thoughts are events in the mind, not orders you must follow.", ""],
  ["THRILLED", "M", "A bright, excited feeling of joy about something good that has happened.", "Thrill can feel light and energizing, with a fizz in the body and a wish to share it. It may be short-lived, which is normal.", "Laughing, talking fast, smiling widely, or calling someone right away to share news.", "Feeling thrilled does not need to last. Enjoy it when it comes, and talk with a doctor if the highs keep getting bigger or cause harm.", ""],
  ["TIMELESS PRESENCE", "U", "A calm sense of being fully here, without the pull of past regrets or future worries.", "Time can seem to slow, and the mind can settle into the moment. Ordinary things, such as a meal or a walk, may feel fuller.", "Listening closely, moving slowly, and pausing to notice the surroundings.", "Presence is not the same as ignoring the future. It means you can plan from a calm place rather than from fear.", ""],
  ["TIMELINE INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of placing painful and good memories in order, to see one's life as a whole.", "Looking at your life as a timeline can bring relief, or it can stir up old pain. Go slowly, and stop when it feels like too much.", "Drawing a life timeline, writing dated notes, or talking through past events in order with a therapist.", "This is not about forcing a neat story or forgiving everything. It helps the past feel more like the past, with a clear place in your story.", ""],
  ["TINGLING SENSATIONS (PARESTHESIA)", "B", "Prickling, buzzing, or numb feelings in the skin, which doctors call paresthesia.", "The skin may feel like it has pins and needles or is asleep. Often it passes, but lasting numbness deserves a doctor's check.", "Rubbing the hands or feet, shaking them out, or changing position often to ease the feeling.", "Tingling often comes from pressure, posture, or a shortage of certain vitamins. Sudden weakness, trouble speaking, or a drooping face needs emergency care.", ""],
  ["TITRATION MASTERY", "K", "A skill of facing hard memories in small, manageable doses, with time to settle between.", "Pacing can feel safer and more hopeful, because you stay in control of how much you face. Progress may feel slow, and that is fine.", "Taking short breaks during hard talks, pausing to breathe, or choosing when to continue.", "Titration does not mean avoiding trauma forever. It is a way to approach it at a pace the body and mind can handle.", ""],
  ["TOMOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of surgery or of medical procedures that cut into the body.", "Thinking of an operation can bring dread, a racing heart, or the urge to cancel. The fear can be so strong that needed care gets delayed.", "Avoiding appointments, delaying surgery, or needing a lot of reassurance before a procedure.", "Fear of surgery is common and understandable. Telling your surgical team about it lets them explain each step and plan support.", ""],
  ["TORMENTOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with torment, suffering, and pain, in stories, images, or thoughts.", "The pull can feel thrilling, dark, or hard to look away from. It can also bring guilt, confusion, or a sense of being out of control.", "Seeking out cruel stories or videos, or describing others' pain with interest or amusement.", "Dark stories are common in art and film, and curiosity about them is not wrongdoing. Enjoying real suffering, or wanting to cause it, is a serious concern that deserves honest help.", ""],
  ["TOXIC POSITIVITY", "T", "The habit of insisting on positivity, which brushes aside real pain and makes others hide feelings.", "Being told to smile through pain can feel lonely and invalidating. It can leave you guilty for struggling at all.", "Quick reassurance such as 'everything happens for a reason', or changing the subject when someone is sad.", "Hope and positive thinking are helpful when they make room for hard feelings. The problem is using positivity to silence pain.", ""],
  ["TOXIC SHAME", "M", "A deep, painful sense that you are fundamentally bad, flawed, or not good enough.", "Shame can make you want to hide, go quiet, or disappear. It can make even kind words feel untrue or undeserved.", "Withdrawing from others, apologizing too much, or harsh self-criticism after small mistakes.", "Toxic shame is a painful feeling, not a fact about your worth. It can be questioned and softened, often with trusted support over time.", ""],
  ["TOXOMANIAC", "V", "A strong, repeated craving for drugs or other harmful substances, even when they cause damage.", "The urge can feel louder than your own plans or fears. Relief comes quickly, then the craving returns and may feel stronger.", "Frequent use despite harm, hiding use, or struggling to cut back even when trying.", "A craving is a sign of a hard pattern, not a lack of character. Change is possible with medical care, counseling, and support groups.", ""],
  ["TRAGOPHILE", "F", "A deep love of goats, and a joy in their company.", "Goats can bring warmth, humor, and a sense of connection. Time with them can feel lighthearted and calming.", "Spending time at farms, collecting goat photos, or keeping goats as companions.", "Loving animals is healthy and grounding. Goats have real needs for space, food, and medical care, so look after them well.", ""],
  ["TRANQUIL", "M", "A calm, quiet state of mind, with little agitation or pressure.", "The body feels slower and softer, and thoughts drift without pulling you along. Tranquility can feel like breathing room.", "Relaxed posture, steady speech, and an unhurried pace.", "Calm is not the same as numbness or avoidance. It is a state you can return to, and it is healthy to enjoy it.", ""],
  ["TRANQUIL MENTAL CLARITY", "U", "A clear, settled mind that can see situations without much noise or confusion.", "Thoughts feel orderly and easier to sort. Decisions can feel less rushed, and worries seem smaller.", "Speaking plainly, pausing before answering, and sorting tasks in a steady order.", "Clarity is not certainty. It means seeing the situation more calmly, even when the answer is still unclear.", ""],
  ["TRANQUIL SELF-MASTERY", "U", "Steady self-control that stays calm, even when emotions run high.", "You can notice strong feelings without being swept away. There is room to choose your next step.", "Taking a breath before replying, speaking evenly, and stepping away when needed.", "Self-mastery is not suppressing feelings. It is choosing how to act on them, and it grows with practice.", ""],
  ["TRANSCENDENT STILLNESS", "U", "A deep stillness that feels larger than the everyday self, and quietly peaceful.", "Time may seem to fall away, and the mind can feel open and spacious. Many people describe it as a sense of connection with something greater.", "Long, quiet pauses, a soft gaze, and a slower pace of speech or movement.", "It can come from meditation, nature, prayer, or rest, and no one path is required. It is a welcome state, not a test of spiritual progress.", ""],
  ["TRANSFORMED", "U", "A lasting change for the better after a hard time, with a new way of living.", "Looking back, the change can feel real and earned, with pride and some tenderness toward the past self. Growth often comes in waves.", "Choosing differently under pressure, setting new boundaries, or speaking about the past with honesty.", "Transformation does not erase the hard time, and it does not mean you are finished changing. It is a process that can continue.", ""],
  ["TRANSVESTIC FETISHISM", "F", "A sexual interest linked to wearing clothing that is associated with another gender.", "Some people feel arousal or comfort from this interest, and it can bring secrecy or shame. Feelings about it can change over time.", "Often private, shown in hidden clothing, private searches, or fantasies.", "Wearing clothing linked to another gender is not a disorder in itself. It is not the same as being transgender.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["TRAUMA", "M", "Lasting emotional injury from an event that overwhelmed a person's ability to cope.", "Trauma can leave the body on alert, with sudden fear, numbness, or irritability. Old memories can feel present, even when the danger has passed.", "Startling easily, avoiding reminders, or having trouble sleeping and trusting others.", "Trauma is a normal reaction to overwhelming events, not a weakness or a personal failure. Recovery is possible with time, support, and evidence-based therapy.", ""],
  ["TRAUMA BONDING", "R", "A strong emotional attachment that forms in cycles of harm and kindness.", "The bond can feel intense, confusing, and hard to leave, even when it hurts. Relief after kindness can feel like proof of love.", "Going back to someone who hurts you, defending them, or feeling unable to leave despite harm.", "This bond is a common response to unpredictable cycles of harm and kindness. It is not a sign of weakness or poor judgment, and leaving is often hard.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps."],
  ["TRAUMA DUMPING", "V", "Sharing painful trauma details with others in full, often without asking whether they can listen.", "Talking about the past can bring short relief, followed by shame or regret. Listeners may feel overwhelmed, and the speaker may feel more alone afterward.", "Sudden long stories of past pain in casual settings, with little space for the listener to respond.", "Wanting to be heard is a human need, and your pain matters. The pattern is about timing and care, and therapy can help you find safer places to share.", ""],
  ["TRAUMA NIGHTMARES", "D", "Repeated bad dreams that replay or echo a past traumatic event.", "Waking from these dreams can bring a racing heart, sweating, or a sense that the danger is still here. Sleep may start to feel unsafe.", "Restless sleep, crying out, sudden waking, or avoiding bed and sleep.", "Nightmares after trauma are common and can ease with treatment. They are not a sign that you are weak or that the past will repeat.", ""],
  ["TRAUMA REENACTMENT", "R", "Repeating painful patterns from the past in present relationships or situations, often without awareness.", "You may feel pulled toward familiar roles, even when they hurt. The pull can feel automatic, and then regret or confusion follows.", "Choosing similar partners or conflicts again and again, or returning to the same harmful dynamic.", "Repeating a pattern does not mean you chose to be hurt, and it is not a personal failing. It is a learned response that can change with support.", ""],
  ["TRAUMA SYMPTOMS", "D", "Signs of trauma, such as flashbacks, avoidance, jumpiness, and strong emotional reactions.", "Symptoms can feel constant or come in waves, and they can make ordinary life feel exhausting. Many people feel on edge or numb.", "Startling easily, avoiding certain places or people, or seeming withdrawn or irritable.", "Many trauma symptoms are the mind and body trying to protect you. They can ease with treatment, such as trauma-focused therapy.", ""],
  ["TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY RELATED DISORDERS", "B", "Changes in thinking, mood, or behavior that can follow a blow or jolt to the head.", "Concentration, memory, or emotions may feel different, and the change can be frustrating. Some symptoms may appear days or months later.", "Forgetfulness, irritability, trouble with planning, or sleep changes after a head injury.", "Brain injury is a medical matter, not a personal failing. Many people improve with rehabilitation, rest, and careful follow-up.", ""],
  ["TREMBLING", "B", "A small, involuntary shake in the hands or voice, often from stress or fight-or-flight hormones.", "The shake may appear without warning, and it can feel embarrassing in front of others. It often eases as the body calms down.", "Visible shaking in the hands, a wobbly voice, or a jittery movement when under pressure.", "Trembling is a normal body reaction, not a sign of weakness. Persistent shaking, or shaking with other symptoms, needs a doctor's check.", ""],
  ["TREMBLING / SHAKING", "B", "Strong, whole-body shaking that can come with fear, chills, illness, or a medical condition.", "The body may feel out of control, and the shaking can make it hard to speak or move. It may pass in minutes or last longer.", "Shaking of the arms or legs, teeth chattering, or trouble holding objects.", "Shaking can be a normal stress response, but it can also signal a medical problem. If it is new, severe, or comes with confusion or chest pain, get emergency help.", ""],
  ["TRIANGULATION", "C", "Pulling a third person into a two-person conflict to create jealousy, control, or confusion.", "The target may feel anxious, rivalrous, or unsure where they stand. Trust in the people involved can wear down.", "Repeating what one person said to another, comparing people, or seeking allies against someone.", "Talking to a trusted friend about a problem is healthy. Triangulation is a pattern of using others to pressure or split people apart.", ""],
  ["TRIANGULATOR", "R", "A person who habitually pulls others into conflicts, often by passing messages or stirring rivalry.", "People around them may feel confused, defensive, or unsure whom to trust. Relationships can become tense and divided.", "Relaying gossip, comparing people, or using a third person to deliver a message.", "Everyone can slip into this sometimes, especially under stress. The pattern matters when it keeps creating conflict and mistrust.", ""],
  ["TRIANGULOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with drama that pulls a third person into two-way conflicts.", "Stories of rivalry and secrets can feel gripping and exciting. The pull can also make everyday conflicts feel more dramatic than they are.", "Watching or following relationship dramas closely, or enjoying gossip about who is taking sides.", "Interest in human drama is common. It becomes a concern when it turns into stirring conflict between people you know.", ""],
  ["TRICHOTILLOMANIA", "D", "A repeated urge to pull out one's own hair, causing hair loss and distress.", "The urge can build until pulling brings relief, and then regret or shame follows. Some people pull without noticing at all.", "Bald or thin patches, hair in the hands or on the floor, or hiding the hair loss.", "Hair pulling is a real condition, not a lack of willpower. It often responds to habit-reversal therapy with a trained therapist.", ""],
  ["TRIGGER MASTERY", "K", "A practice of noticing triggers, calming the body, and choosing a response on purpose.", "Triggers can feel sudden and overwhelming, and mastery can bring a sense of control. Progress often arrives in small steps.", "Naming what set off a reaction, taking a breath, and choosing a calmer next step.", "Mastery does not mean never being triggered. It means having more room between the trigger and the response.", ""],
  ["TRIGGER REWIRING", "K", "A practice of slowly changing how the mind and body react to reminders of past pain.", "Rewiring can feel uncomfortable at first, then gradually less intense. Small wins can build hope over time.", "Staying with a reminder for a moment, using a grounding cue, and then returning to the present.", "Rewiring is not forcing yourself to feel nothing. It is building new responses, often with help from a trauma-informed therapist.", ""],
  ["TRIMETHYLAMINURIA", "B", "A rare inherited condition in which the body cannot break down a smelly compound made from certain foods.", "The body gives off a strong fishy odor, and this can cause embarrassment and social stress. Many people feel anxious about being around others.", "A strong fishy smell on breath, sweat, or urine that others notice.", "This is not caused by poor hygiene, and washing more often does not fix it. Diet changes and medical care can reduce the odor.", ""],
  ["TRISKAIDEKAPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of the number thirteen, which can lead to avoiding certain dates or places.", "Seeing thirteen can bring unease, a flutter of dread, or a sense that bad luck is near. The feeling can seem silly, but it is real.", "Skipping the thirteenth floor, avoiding certain dates, or shifting plans to avoid the number.", "Many people feel uneasy about thirteen, and the fear has no link to actual danger. The fear is still real to the person who feels it.", ""],
  ["TRIUMPHANT", "M", "A feeling of victory and pride after overcoming something hard or meeting a goal.", "The win can bring a surge of energy, relief, and a strong sense of pride. It may fade over time, which is normal, and it can be worth savoring.", "Raising arms, sharing the news widely, or talking about the achievement with energy.", "Celebrating a win is healthy and well earned. Watch for an intense, endless high with little sleep, and talk with a doctor if that happens.", ""],
  ["TRIVIALIZER", "R", "A person who waves away others' concerns as minor, unimportant, or overblown.", "The other person can feel dismissed, silly, or unheard. Over time, they may stop sharing what matters to them.", "Responses like 'it is no big deal' or 'you are overreacting' when someone raises a concern.", "Some people minimize things to stay calm, and that is not always meant as an insult. The pattern becomes harmful when it keeps important feelings out of reach.", ""],
  ["TROJAN HORSE FAVOR", "C", "A kind-looking favor that hides a secret cost, debt, or obligation to be repaid later.", "The favor can leave you feeling grateful, then cornered when the bill arrives. You may feel guilty about saying no.", "Offering help with strings attached, then reminding you of it later or asking for something in return.", "Most offers of help are genuine, and accepting help is not wrong. Be watchful when a favor comes with hidden terms or pressure.", ""],
  ["TRUST IMPAIRMENT", "T", "A reduced ability to trust others or oneself, often after repeated hurt or betrayal.", "Trust can feel dangerous, and small signs of unreliability can feel huge. You may feel on guard even with people who are safe.", "Testing people repeatedly, checking up on others, or pulling away after small mistakes.", "Caution after betrayal is understandable, and it can ease over time. Trust is rebuilt through consistent behavior, not through forcing yourself to believe people.", ""],
  ["TRUST ISSUES", "R", "A pattern of difficulty trusting others, often rooted in earlier hurt or unreliable care.", "Closeness can feel risky, and you may expect to be let down. Even kind people can feel suspicious at first.", "Hesitating to share feelings, doubting others' motives, or keeping people at a distance.", "Trust issues are common and understandable, especially after hurt. They can soften when people show care over time, and with support.", ""],
  ["TRYPANOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of needles, injections, or blood draws.", "Seeing a needle can bring a racing heart, dizziness, or a wish to run away. The fear can make needed care feel out of reach.", "Looking away from injections, tensing up, or delaying vaccines or blood tests.", "Needle fear is very common, and it does not mean you are weak. Care teams can help with numbing cream, slow positioning, and distraction.", ""],
  ["TRYPOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong unease at clusters of small holes or bumps, such as honeycombs or seed pods.", "Looking at such patterns can bring a creeping, crawling feeling on the skin and a strong wish to look away. The reaction can feel out of proportion.", "Turning away from photos of sponges, honeycombs, or pitted surfaces, or covering the eyes.", "Many people share this reaction, and research on it is still limited. It is real for those who feel it, and it can ease with gradual exposure.", ""],
  ["TUBEROUS SCLEROSIS", "B", "A rare inherited condition that causes noncancerous growths in the brain, skin, heart, or kidneys.", "Symptoms vary widely, and some people feel worry about seizures or learning differences. Support from a specialist team can make a big difference.", "Seizures, skin marks, learning or attention differences, or behavior changes, depending on the person.", "This is a genetic condition, not something a person caused or could easily avoid. Modern care can treat many symptoms and help people live well.", ""],
  ["TURNER SYNDROME", "B", "A genetic condition in some people assigned female at birth, affecting growth, heart, and fertility.", "The person may feel worried about health checks, puberty, or having children. Support from a specialist team can help them plan.", "Slower growth in childhood, delayed puberty, or heart and kidney checks needed over time.", "It is caused by a chromosome difference, not by anything a person did. With regular medical care, many people live full, healthy lives.", ""],
  ["ULTIMATUM", "C", "A demand with a harsh consequence attached, used to force a choice under pressure.", "The other person may feel cornered, afraid, or unable to think clearly. Either choice can bring guilt, and the pressure can make clear thinking hard.", "Saying 'choose me or them', or 'do this now, or else', when there is room to talk.", "A clear boundary can be healthy, and it is not always an ultimatum. The pattern is harmful when pressure replaces honest discussion.", ""],
  ["UMBROPHILE", "F", "A love of shade, shadows, and the cool, dim light of quiet places.", "Shade can feel soothing and private, especially on bright, busy days. It can bring a sense of rest and retreat.", "Choosing shaded seats, walking under trees, or spending time in dim, quiet corners.", "Liking shade is healthy and common. It becomes a concern only if it leads to long isolation or avoiding daylight, which affects mood.", ""],
  ["UNCONDITIONAL SELF-LOVE", "U", "Accepting and caring for yourself without requiring achievement or perfection first.", "Self-acceptance can feel warm and steadying, and it can soften harsh inner criticism. It may come and go, and that is normal.", "Speaking kindly to yourself after a mistake, resting without guilt, and asking for help.", "Self-love is not self-indulgence or pretending to be perfect. It means treating yourself with the same fairness you would offer a good friend.", ""],
  ["UNFAVORABLE COMPARISON", "T", "A habit of judging yourself harshly by measuring your life against others' highlights.", "Comparisons can bring envy, shame, or a sense of falling behind. They often feel true, even though they use an unfair picture.", "Frequent remarks like 'they have it all' or 'I am behind everyone', or quiet sulking after seeing others' news.", "Everyone compares sometimes, and it is human. The problem is when comparisons keep you from seeing your own progress clearly.", ""],
  ["UNSHAKABLE", "U", "Steady and hard to knock off course, even when pressure or criticism arrives.", "Inner steadiness can feel grounded and calm, with less need to react. You can still feel strong emotions, and stay steady anyway.", "Staying composed in a tense meeting, answering criticism calmly, and keeping plans on track.", "Being unshakable does not mean never struggling. It means returning to your center after a hard moment.", ""],
  ["UNSHAKABLE CENTEREDNESS", "U", "A settled inner center that stays put when life around you feels chaotic.", "Centeredness can feel like solid ground under your feet. Noise around you may still be loud, but it reaches you less.", "Slow, even speech, a grounded posture, and steady responses in tense moments.", "Centeredness is not indifference to others. It means you stay present and kind without losing your footing.", ""],
  ["UNSHAKABLE INNER CONFIDENCE", "U", "A quiet confidence in your own worth and abilities that does not depend on praise.", "Confidence can feel steady and self-assured, with less fear of others' opinions. It grows when you keep promises to yourself.", "Speaking up calmly, accepting feedback without collapsing, and making decisions with clear reasons.", "Inner confidence is not arrogance or never doubting yourself. It means trusting that you can handle doubt and learn from it.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED", "D", "A general label for real symptoms that do not yet fit one named condition.", "Symptoms can be distressing even without a clear name, and that can feel confusing. A label, even a general one, can help you get the right care.", "Changes in mood, thinking, or behavior that a clinician is still working to understand.", "A general label is not a dismissal. It means the care team is still gathering information to find the best fit.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED ANXIETY DISORDER", "D", "A label for anxiety that causes real distress but does not fit one named anxiety condition.", "Worry or tension can feel constant, with a body that seems always on alert. The strain can spill into sleep, work, and relationships.", "Frequent reassurance seeking, avoiding ordinary tasks, or visible restlessness and fatigue.", "Anxiety is treatable even when it does not match a textbook label. Care can be built around what you actually experience.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED DISSOCIATIVE DISORDER", "D", "A label for disconnection from self, memory, or surroundings that does not fit one named condition.", "You may feel far away from your body, or unsure of time, identity, or what is real. These episodes can be frightening and exhausting.", "Going blank for stretches of time, seeming absent, or having gaps in memory.", "Dissociation is a way the mind protects itself under stress or after trauma. It is treatable, and specialized therapy can help.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED FEEDING OR EATING DISORDER", "D", "A label for eating problems that cause distress but do not fit one named eating condition.", "Food and eating can feel stressful, secret, or out of control, and thoughts about food can take up much of the day. Eating can bring guilt, fear, or relief.", "Skipping meals, eating in secret, rigid food rules, or distress around certain foods.", "Eating problems are serious health concerns, not a matter of willpower or self-control. Recovery is possible with medical and emotional care.", "A doctor or eating-disorder specialist can help. In the US, call or text 988 any time you are struggling."],
  ["UNSPECIFIED MENTAL DISORDER", "D", "A label for mental health symptoms that cause real distress but fit no single named condition.", "Symptoms can feel confusing and hard to describe, which can be lonely. Many people feel relieved when they finally have a plan.", "Changes in mood, sleep, focus, or daily function that a clinician is still exploring.", "A general label is not a sign that nothing can be done. It usually means more information is needed to choose the right care.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED NEURODEVELOPMENTAL DISORDER", "D", "A label for developmental differences in learning, attention, language, or movement that fit no single named condition.", "Learning, focus, or communication may feel harder than it does for others, and that can be frustrating. Support can make everyday tasks feel more manageable.", "Difficulty with reading, focus, or social timing, or strong needs for routine and structure.", "This label does not mean low ability. It means a person's strengths and needs are still being understood, and support can help.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A label for long-lasting patterns in feeling, thinking, and relating that do not fit one named type.", "Relationships and self-image can feel unstable, and emotions may shift quickly. Living this way can be exhausting for the person and those close to them.", "Strong reactions in close relationships, or long-standing difficulty with trust and self-image.", "Personality patterns are not a fixed verdict on a person. Therapy focused on skills and relationships can help people change how they respond.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED SOMATIC SYMPTOM AND RELATED DISORDER", "D", "A label for real physical symptoms that cause strain, with stress and thoughts playing a large part.", "The symptoms can feel exhausting and frightening, and worry about them can grow. The body and mind affect each other closely.", "Frequent visits to doctors, concern about pain or illness, or limiting daily activities.", "The symptoms are real, even when tests find no single cause. Care that treats both body and stress usually works best.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED SUBSTANCE-RELATED AND ADDICTIVE DISORDER", "D", "A label for problems with substances or repeated behaviors that do not fit one named disorder.", "Cravings or losing control can bring shame, secrecy, and a wish to stop that feels hard to act on. Relief can come, then the pattern returns.", "Using more than planned, repeated attempts to cut back, or continuing despite harm.", "Addiction is a health condition, not a moral failing, and it responds to care. Treatment options are available, and no one has to manage it alone.", ""],
  ["UNSPECIFIED TRAUMA- AND STRESSOR-RELATED DISORDER", "D", "A label for trauma or stress symptoms that cause real distress but fit no single named condition.", "Stress reactions can feel constant or come in waves, with sleep and focus hit hard. The past can feel close, even when things seem calmer now.", "Jumpiness, avoiding reminders, irritability, or strong reactions to ordinary stress.", "Stress and trauma responses are normal reactions to hard events, and they can ease with support. A label helps the care team choose the right approach.", ""],
  ["UNWAVERING", "U", "Steady in purpose and care, even when circumstances change or doubts appear.", "Steadiness can feel calm and reliable, a quiet sense that you know where you stand. It does not require feeling certain every day.", "Keeping commitments through hard weeks, staying kind under pressure, and returning to goals.", "Unwavering does not mean stubborn or unwilling to adjust. You can stay committed to values while changing your methods.", ""],
  ["UNWAVERING SELF-MASTERY", "U", "Steady, reliable control over your own choices, habits, and reactions.", "Self-mastery can feel grounded and proud, and it makes hard choices feel clearer. Setbacks still happen, but they teach more than they break.", "Keeping routines even when tired, pausing before reacting, and following through on plans.", "Self-mastery is not perfect control or harsh self-discipline. It grows from patience, practice, and forgiving slips.", ""],
  ["UPLIFTED", "M", "A lighter, brighter mood that lifts the spirit after something kind or hopeful.", "The heart can feel warm and open, and the day may seem more possible. Uplift often fades, and that is natural.", "Smiling more, speaking warmly, or sharing encouragement with others.", "Feeling uplifted is good, and it does not need to be explained away. If elevated moods come with little sleep or risky choices, talk with a doctor.", ""],
  ["UPLIFTING", "U", "Something that lifts the spirit, such as music, nature, kindness, or a meaningful story.", "Uplifting experiences can bring warmth, hope, and a sense of connection. Their effect can last for hours or days.", "Sharing a song, passing on encouragement, or spending time outdoors with people you enjoy.", "Uplifting things are not a cure for pain, and they do not need to fix everything. They work best alongside rest, support, and care.", ""],
  ["UROPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in urine, which can bring shame or confusion.", "The interest can feel private, confusing, or embarrassing, and it may come with strong urges. Many people feel relief when they can talk about it honestly.", "Usually private, shown in fantasies, private searches, or secret habits.", "Having the interest is not the same as acting on it. Acting without clear, ongoing consent harms others.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["VANITY", "R", "An excessive focus on one's appearance, status, or how one is seen by others.", "Image can feel like a constant job, with worry about how others see you. Praise brings a lift, and criticism can sting badly.", "Frequent talk about achievements, constant selfies, or seeking compliments.", "Caring about how you present yourself is normal and can be healthy. Vanity becomes a problem when self-worth depends on being admired.", ""],
  ["VASCULAR DEMENTIA", "B", "Memory and thinking decline, called dementia, caused by reduced blood flow to the brain.", "The decline can come in steps after small strokes, rather than as a steady slide. Confusion and memory lapses can be frightening for the person and family.", "Forgetting recent events, slower thinking, trouble with planning, or changes in walking.", "This is a medical condition, not a normal part of aging or a personal failing. Caring for blood pressure, heart health, and stroke risk can help protect thinking.", ""],
  ["VEILED THREAT", "C", "A threat hidden inside polite or indirect words, meant to frighten or control.", "The message can leave you feeling uneasy, watchful, and unsure how to respond. The fear can linger long after the words are spoken.", "Hints such as 'you would not want to find out what happens', or remarks that sound harmless but feel menacing.", "A veiled threat is still a threat, even if it sounds calm or joking. Trust your sense of unease and seek support.", ""],
  ["VENGEFUL", "M", "A strong, burning urge to get even with someone who has hurt you.", "The anger can feel hot and consuming, and it may keep replaying the hurt. Fantasies of payback can bring brief relief, then more tension.", "Brooding, talking about revenge, or looking for ways to make someone pay.", "Wanting justice after harm is human. Revenge tends to keep the hurt going, and it can bring new harm to you as well.", ""],
  ["VENTRAL VAGAL ACTIVATION", "B", "A calm, connected state that some therapists link to the vagus nerve, which runs from brain to body.", "The body can feel safe, warm, and able to rest, with breathing that slows naturally. Connection with others can feel easier in this state.", "Relaxed face and shoulders, slower speech, and easy eye contact.", "The idea is a helpful way to think about calm, but the nerve science behind it is still debated. Calm can be built through breathing, rest, and kind connection, whatever the theory.", ""],
  ["VERBIGERATING", "D", "Repeating the same words, phrases, or movements over and over, with no clear purpose.", "The person may feel stuck in the repetition, or may not notice it at all. Others may feel worried, confused, or unsure how to help.", "Saying the same phrase many times, repeating a gesture, or getting stuck on one word.", "It can be a sign of a serious condition, such as psychosis (losing touch with what is real). It needs prompt medical care, and it is a symptom, not a choice.", "A doctor or psychiatrist should check this soon. Please call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["VIBRANT", "U", "Full of lively energy, color, and enthusiasm for life.", "Vibrancy can feel bright and alive, with a sense that things are worth doing. It can make ordinary days feel more textured.", "Animated talk, quick smiles, and willingness to try new things.", "Vibrancy is not the same as being always on or never tired. Rest and quiet are part of a healthy, lively life.", ""],
  ["VIBRANT INNER JOY", "U", "A steady, bright joy that comes from within, not only from outside events.", "Inner joy can feel warm and buoyant, with a sense of being at home in yourself. It can return after hard times, sometimes softly.", "Quiet contentment, gentle humor, and sharing small delights with others.", "Inner joy is not a requirement to feel happy all the time. It grows through meaning, rest, connection, and practice.", ""],
  ["VIBRANT LIFE CELEBRATION", "U", "Marking and enjoying good moments in life, big or small, with gratitude and joy.", "Celebrating can feel light and connected, and it helps good memories stay vivid. It also makes hard times feel less all-consuming.", "Sharing milestones with friends, hosting small gatherings, or saying thank you often.", "Celebration does not require spending money or having a big event. A simple toast, a note, or a walk can count.", ""],
  ["VIBRANT LIFE FORCE", "U", "A strong, energetic drive to live fully, grow, and engage with the world.", "This energy can feel like a current pulling you forward, with curiosity and hope. It can rise and fall with rest and health.", "Starting projects, pursuing new skills, and showing up for others with energy.", "Strong energy is good, but it is not endless. Watch for signs of exhaustion or restless nights, and rest when needed.", ""],
  ["VIBRANT SELF-EMPOWERMENT", "U", "Taking active, confident ownership of your choices, voice, and next steps.", "Empowerment can feel strong and clear, and it makes it easier to speak up. It may be uneven, with some days harder than others.", "Asking for what you need, setting clear limits, and making plans with others.", "Self-empowerment does not mean doing everything alone. Asking for help is also a choice you can make with strength.", ""],
  ["VIBRANT VITALITY", "U", "Lively physical and mental energy that supports a full, active life.", "Vitality can feel buoyant, with a body ready to move and a mind ready to engage. It usually grows with sleep, good food, movement, and connection.", "Brisk walking, quick movement, bright attention, and an eagerness to join in.", "Vitality is not about being perfect or never feeling tired. It is supported by rest, good care, and kindness to yourself.", ""],
  ["VICARIOUS TRAUMA", "M", "Emotional distress from repeatedly hearing or seeing other people's trauma.", "Stories of harm can linger, bringing sadness, dread, or a sense of helplessness. You may feel changed by others' pain.", "Irritability, emotional withdrawal, or sleep trouble among people who support others for work.", "Caring deeply for people who have been hurt is admirable, and it can take a toll. It is a normal reaction that needs care, not a sign of failure.", ""],
  ["VICTIM FLIP", "C", "Turning the tables so the person who was harmed is cast as the wrongdoer.", "The person who raised the harm may feel confused, guilty, or silenced. They may start doubting their own memory.", "Saying 'you are the one who is cruel' right after someone describes being hurt.", "Taking responsibility is healthy, but a victim flip avoids accountability by blaming the harmed person. Clear records and trusted support can help.", ""],
  ["VICTIM MENTALITY", "T", "A habit of seeing yourself as always wronged, with little sense of having any choice.", "Feeling powerless can bring anger, bitterness, or sadness that does not ease. Others may feel pushed away by constant hurt.", "Frequent talk about being treated unfairly, and little talk about what might be changed.", "Feeling wronged can be real, and past harm deserves to be heard. The pattern becomes a problem when it blocks any sense of choice or action.", ""],
  ["VINDICTIVE", "R", "A habit of holding grudges and seeking to punish those who have wronged you.", "Grudges can feel like protection, but they keep old hurt alive. Rumination can take up time and energy that you want for other things.", "Keeping score, reminding others of past wrongs, or acting to make someone pay.", "Wanting fairness is healthy. The pattern is harmful when punishment becomes the goal and it keeps conflicts going for years.", ""],
  ["VISIONARY", "U", "Able to imagine a better future clearly and to work toward it step by step.", "Vision can bring hope and energy, and a sense of purpose that steadies the day. Imagining the future can also feel uncertain.", "Describing big goals with clarity, planning small steps, and inviting others into the picture.", "A visionary is not always certain or right. Good vision stays open to feedback and changes course when needed.", ""],
  ["VIVACIOUS", "U", "Lively, spirited, and full of energy in conversation and daily life.", "Vivacity can feel bright and easy, with a pleasant sense of being fully present. Energy may dip sometimes, and that is part of living.", "Animated speech, expressive gestures, and quick, warm laughter.", "Vivacity is not the same as talking constantly or being the center of attention. Quiet people can be just as lively inside.", ""],
  ["VOIDOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with empty spaces, blank voids, and the idea of nothingness.", "Emptiness can feel calming or eerie, and it may draw curiosity. For some, it can echo a sense of emptiness inside, which deserves attention.", "Staring into dark or empty spaces, collecting images of voids, or talking often about emptiness.", "Interest in quiet emptiness is common and can be peaceful. It becomes a concern when it brings persistent hopelessness, which deserves support.", ""],
  ["VOLATILE", "R", "Quick to shift from calm to intense anger or emotion, often without much warning.", "Those around them may feel on edge, careful with words, or afraid of the next outburst. Calm can return quickly, which can be confusing.", "Sudden shouting, slamming doors, or abrupt changes in tone during a disagreement.", "Strong feelings are part of being human. The pattern becomes harmful when outbursts hurt others or leave people afraid.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps."],
  ["VOYEURISM", "F", "A sexual interest in watching people who do not know they are being watched.", "The pull can feel secret and exciting, and it can bring guilt or shame. Fantasy can keep the urge going, even when the person wants to stop.", "Secret watching, hidden cameras, or repeated private searches for images of others.", "Watching people without their agreement is a serious privacy violation, and it is illegal in many places. Help is available before harm happens.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["VULNERABILITY WEAPONIZATION", "C", "Using someone's private fears, openness, or past pain against them to control or hurt them.", "The target may feel exposed, ashamed, and afraid to share again. Trust can break down, and they may start to hide important feelings.", "Bringing up a past secret during an argument, mocking a fear, or threatening to share private details.", "Sharing vulnerability is healthy when it is met with care. The harm is in using it as a weapon, and it deserves clear limits and support.", ""],
  ["VULNERABLE NARCISSIST", "R", "A person with fragile self-esteem who seeks admiration through hurt, sensitivity, or withdrawal.", "They may feel easily wounded, and they may need constant reassurance. Others may feel they can never say the right thing.", "Sulking after being overlooked, hinting at being misunderstood, or sharp reactions to small criticisms.", "Feeling hurt is not the same as this pattern, and everyone's feelings deserve care. The pattern becomes harmful when the need for admiration shapes how others are treated.", ""],
  ["WALKING ON EGGSHELLS", "R", "Feeling you must tread very carefully to avoid upsetting someone.", "You may feel constant tension, scanning moods and words before you speak. Relief comes only when things are calm, and it does not last.", "Choosing words carefully, avoiding topics, or quickly apologizing to smooth things over.", "Some care is normal, but constant fear is not. It can point to a harmful dynamic that deserves outside support.", "The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. A trusted person, counselor, or advocate can help plan next steps."],
  ["WALL PUSH OR GROUNDING STOMP", "K", "A simple body-based grounding practice that uses firm pushing or stomping to release tension.", "Pushing against a wall or stomping can bring a sense of solid contact and release. The body feels more present afterward.", "Pressing both palms against a wall, or stamping feet slowly while breathing out.", "This is a support tool, not treatment for a crisis. If strong feelings keep building, reach out to a counselor or trusted person.", ""],
  ["WARM DRINK RITUAL", "K", "A calming routine of making and slowly sipping a warm drink with full attention.", "Warmth in the hands and throat can feel soothing, and the ritual can signal that it is time to slow down. Small pleasures can steady the day.", "Holding a mug with both hands, sitting quietly, and taking slow sips.", "A warm drink is a comfort, not a treatment for depression, anxiety, or pain. If feelings stay heavy, a doctor or counselor can help.", ""],
  ["WEAKENED IMMUNE SYSTEM", "B", "A body defense that works less well than usual, so infections can be harder to fight.", "Being sick more often can feel frustrating, worrying, and tiring. Some people feel anxious about catching illnesses in daily life.", "Frequent colds or infections, slow healing, or needing extra care when sick.", "A weak immune system is a medical matter, and it can have many causes, including illness or medicines. Doctors can test and support it.", ""],
  ["WEAPONIZED INCOMPETENCE", "C", "Pretending not to know how to do a task so that someone else keeps taking it on.", "The person doing the work may feel resentful, overloaded, and taken for granted. Trust in fairness can wear down over time.", "Saying 'I just cannot do it' when a task is expected, then handing it back with a sigh.", "Some people truly struggle with a task, and asking for help is fair. The pattern is about avoiding responsibility while someone else pays the cost.", ""],
  ["WEIGHTED BLANKET DEEP PRESSURE", "K", "Gentle, even pressure from a weighted blanket, which many people find calming.", "Steady pressure can feel grounding, like being held, and it may ease restlessness. Some people sleep more easily with it.", "Choosing a heavy blanket for rest, settling down under it, or breathing more calmly.", "Weighted blankets are unsafe for babies and young children. Anyone with breathing problems should ask a doctor before using one.", ""],
  ["WERNER SYNDROME", "B", "A rare inherited condition that causes signs of premature aging in adulthood.", "Early changes in skin, hair, or eyes can feel distressing, and the person may feel worried about the future. Support from a specialist can help plan care.", "Early graying or thinning hair, skin changes, eye clouding called cataracts, or stiff joints.", "This is a genetic condition, not a result of lifestyle or poor care. Specialist teams can monitor and treat complications as they arise.", ""],
  ["WHATABOUTISM", "T", "Answering a criticism by pointing to someone else's faults instead of addressing it.", "The person raising the concern may feel dismissed or confused. The topic stays unresolved, and frustration can build.", "Replies like 'what about when you did that' or 'everyone does it' after a complaint.", "Pointing out someone else's fault can be fair in some cases. The pattern becomes unhelpful when it is used to avoid the original issue.", ""],
  ["WHOLENESS LEGACY", "U", "The lasting positive impact of living with integrity, care, and a sense of being whole.", "Thinking of the legacy you leave can bring meaning, pride, and humility. It can feel calming to live in line with what matters.", "Keeping promises, mentoring others, and sharing knowledge or care that lasts.", "Legacy is not about fame or perfection. It grows through ordinary kindness, honesty, and repair after mistakes.", ""],
  ["WILLIAMS SYNDROME", "B", "A rare genetic condition that affects heart and blood vessels, learning, and social understanding.", "Strengths in language and friendliness can sit alongside challenges with math, space, or focus. Each person's experience is different.", "Often a warm, sociable manner, strong verbal skills, and trouble with tasks that need spatial sense.", "This is a genetic difference, not a choice or a result of poor parenting. Heart and blood pressure checks are important, along with learning support.", ""],
  ["WINDOW OF TOLERANCE EXPANSION", "K", "A practice of slowly widening the range of stress you can feel and still stay present.", "As the window grows, strong feelings can feel less like emergencies. Progress is often small, and it builds over many weeks.", "Staying calm in a tough meeting, recovering faster after upset, or naming feelings without freezing or lashing out.", "The window of tolerance is a helpful way to describe stress zones, and it is not a precise measurement. Expansion comes from safe, steady practice, not from pushing yourself.", ""],
  ["WISE", "U", "Good judgment built from experience, care, and an honest look at what matters.", "Wisdom can feel calm and clear, with less rush to prove something. It often comes after mistakes and reflection.", "Pausing before deciding, asking good questions, and admitting what you do not know.", "Wisdom is not being old or always right. It is a practice that grows with reflection and humility.", ""],
  ["WISE COMPASSIONATE OBSERVER", "K", "A calm, caring stance from which you watch your own experience without judging it.", "Watching with care can create space between you and strong feelings. It can soften self-criticism and make hard moments easier to hold.", "Pausing to notice what is happening, speaking gently to yourself, and responding rather than reacting.", "Observing is not the same as ignoring pain or pretending it is fine. It is a way to see clearly and respond with kindness.", ""],
  ["WISE DISCERNMENT", "U", "Clear judgment that tells apart what truly matters from what only seems urgent.", "Discernment can feel steady and grounded, with less noise in the decision. It may still take time and careful thought.", "Asking which issues are important, letting minor concerns pass, and weighing options with care.", "Discernment is not the same as overthinking or endless analysis. It is a practical skill that improves with calm reflection and feedback.", ""],
  ["WISE FLEXIBILITY", "U", "The ability to adapt your approach while staying loyal to your core values.", "Adapting can feel freeing, with less fear of change. It can also bring grief for old plans, which deserves room.", "Trying a different plan when one fails, listening to new information, and adjusting calmly.", "Flexibility does not mean dropping principles or going along with anything. It means changing methods while keeping what matters.", ""],
  ["WISE INTEGRATION", "U", "Bringing together thoughts, feelings, and experiences into a clear, coherent whole.", "Pieces that once felt scattered can start to make sense together. The process can feel quiet and meaningful.", "Speaking about past and present with coherence, and linking choices to values.", "Integration does not mean every conflict is solved or every memory is comfortable. It means holding the parts together with more clarity.", ""],
  ["WISE SELF-COMPASSION", "U", "Treating yourself with honest kindness, especially when you struggle or make mistakes.", "Self-compassion can feel warm and steadying, and it can make it easier to try again. Some days it may feel unnatural at first.", "Speaking to yourself kindly after a setback, owning mistakes, and making a repair plan.", "Self-compassion is not self-pity or lowering your standards. It is combining warmth with honesty about what needs to change.", ""],
  ["WITHDRAWAL REFLEX (TO PAIN)", "B", "The body's fast, automatic pull away from something painful, before the mind catches up.", "The movement can happen in a flash, and you may only notice it afterward. It is a protective response, and it is normal.", "Quickly pulling a hand or foot back from heat or a sharp touch.", "This reflex protects the body and is not a choice or a sign of weakness. Persistent pain or numbness should be checked by a doctor.", ""],
  ["WITHDRAWN", "M", "Pulled back from people, activities, and conversations, often with a sense of shrinking inward.", "Withdrawing can feel like a relief at first, then lonelier as time passes. Energy and interest may seem far away.", "Canceling plans, answering messages late, or spending long periods alone.", "Needing space can be healthy, but ongoing withdrawal can signal low mood or stress. Reaching out, even briefly, can help.", ""],
  ["WORD SALAD", "D", "Speech made of jumbled words and phrases that do not connect into clear meaning.", "The speaker may feel their meaning is clear, while listeners feel lost. This can be frightening for everyone involved.", "Sentences that drift into unrelated words, with few clear links between ideas.", "It can come with psychosis, which means losing touch with what is real, or with dementia. It is a medical sign, not a choice or a character flaw.", "A doctor or psychiatrist should check this soon. Please call or text 988 in the US, and call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["WORD SALADER", "C", "A person who uses tangled, circular arguments to confuse others and avoid accountability.", "Listeners may feel dizzy, frustrated, or unsure what was agreed. Conversations can end with them doubting their own memory or judgment.", "Answering with long, looping talk that never addresses the question, then insisting the matter is settled.", "Rambling under stress is different from confusing people on purpose. The pattern matters when confusion is used to avoid responsibility.", ""],
  ["WORKAHOLISM", "V", "A compulsive pattern of working far beyond what is healthy, often at the cost of rest and relationships.", "Work can feel like the only safe place, and stopping can bring restlessness or guilt. Rest may feel undeserved.", "Working late and on weekends, skipping meals or sleep for tasks, and missing time with loved ones.", "Hard work and dedication are valuable, and busy seasons happen. The pattern becomes harmful when rest is always sacrificed and health or relationships suffer.", ""],
  ["WORRY TIME SCHEDULING", "K", "A practice of setting aside a fixed daily window to think through worries.", "Having a set time can make worries feel more manageable, since they wait their turn. Some people feel relief at the end of the window.", "Keeping a daily worry notebook, postponing a worry to a set time, or writing it down for later.", "This practice is not ignoring worries, and it is not a way to avoid problems. It helps you face them with a clear plan and a set time to rest.", ""],
  ["WOUNDED INNER CHILD", "T", "The idea that old childhood hurt still shapes how you feel and react today.", "Old pain can surface in adult moments, bringing sudden sadness, shame, or a need for reassurance. It can feel young and raw.", "Strong reactions to small slights, needing constant comfort, or reacting as if an old wound is open again.", "Inner child is a metaphor for old pain, not a literal part of you. Its scientific support is limited, but many people find the language helpful.", ""],
  ["WRATH", "M", "Intense, burning anger that can feel all-consuming and lead to actions you later regret.", "Wrath can feel hot, sharp, and urgent, with a strong wish to strike back. The body may feel tight and flooded with energy.", "Raised voice, clenched jaw, slammed objects, or a cold, hard stare during conflict.", "Anger signals that something matters, and it is a normal emotion. Acting on wrath can cause lasting harm, so pause and get support when it rises.", ""],
  ["XENOMANIAC", "F", "An excessive admiration for things, people, or customs from other countries.", "Foreign things can feel exciting and more valuable than what is familiar. The pull can leave you feeling disconnected from your own roots.", "Preferring foreign brands, dismissing local customs, or rushing to adopt every new trend from abroad.", "Curiosity about other cultures is healthy and enriching. It becomes a concern when it pushes away your own life or belittles the people around you.", ""],
  ["XENOPHILIA", "U", "A warm openness to people, languages, and customs from other places.", "Curiosity about other people can feel warm and expanding, and it can lessen fear of the unfamiliar. It can make the world feel larger.", "Asking questions about other cultures, learning phrases, and welcoming newcomers with interest.", "Xenophilia is different from xenophobia, which is fear or dislike of outsiders. Openness is healthy, and it does not mean rejecting your own identity.", ""],
  ["XERODERMA PIGMENTOSUM", "B", "A rare inherited condition that makes skin extremely sensitive to sunlight and ultraviolet rays.", "Sun exposure can cause painful burns quickly, and the constant need for protection can feel limiting. Families often feel worried for the future.", "Freckle-like spots on sun-exposed skin, sunburn from brief time outdoors, or eye irritation from light.", "It is inherited, not caused by careless sun habits, and it is not contagious. Specialist care, strict sun protection, and regular skin checks can greatly lower risks.", ""],
  ["YAWNING", "B", "A reflex yawn that often comes with tiredness, boredom, or stress.", "A yawn can bring a brief stretch of relief and a sense of the body asking for rest. Sometimes yawning seems to come from nowhere.", "Opening the mouth wide, stretching, or yawning during a conversation or meeting.", "Yawning is normal, and it is not a sign of rudeness or disinterest in every case. Very frequent yawning with sleep trouble is worth discussing with a doctor.", ""],
  ["ZESTFUL", "U", "Full of eager, cheerful energy for life, its small pleasures and its challenges.", "Zest can feel bright and playful, with a readiness to try things. It can ebb and flow, and that is part of being human.", "Enthusiastic greetings, eager questions, and enjoyment of everyday activities.", "Zest is not constant happiness or a requirement to be upbeat all the time. It is a quality that can be nurtured with rest and care.", ""],
  ["ZOOMANIAC", "F", "An excessive, consuming interest in animals, often collecting or keeping many of them.", "Animals can bring deep comfort and purpose, and the urge to help can feel strong. The pull can become hard to limit.", "Keeping many animals in a small space, spending money on animals beyond one's means, or turning down other responsibilities.", "Loving animals is healthy, but too many animals can lead to neglect and suffering. Their needs for food, space, and vet care must come first.", ""],
  ["ZOOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in animals, which can cause serious harm to them.", "The interest can feel shameful, confusing, or hard to talk about, and it may bring strong urges. Many people feel isolated by it.", "Usually private, shown in fantasies or private searches, but it can lead to harmful acts.", "Animals cannot consent, so any sexual act with one is abuse. Acting on this interest harms animals, and it is a crime in many places.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["THE GUILTTRIPOPHILE", "C", "A person who often uses guilt trips to steer others and may take pleasure in their effect.", "The person being guilt-tripped may feel responsible for things that are not theirs. They may give in, then feel resentful later.", "Phrases like 'after everything I have done for you', or sulking until someone apologizes.", "Caring about how actions affect others is healthy, and guilt can sometimes be useful. Guilt used as a steering tool is manipulation, and it harms trust.", ""],
  ["THE VORAREPHILE", "F", "A sexual interest in fantasies about eating or being eaten.", "The fantasies can feel intense, private, and hard to explain, and they may bring shame or confusion. Many people feel relief when they talk about them honestly.", "Usually private, shown in stories, art, or private searches.", "Having a fantasy is not the same as acting on it. Acting on it without consent harms others, and that is serious harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
];
